-- title:
--   Zero scheme of the canonical section of mathcal O_X(D)
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and let $I$ be an ideal sheaf datum on $X$ (a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals recorded by its ideal $I.\mathrm{ideal}\,U \subseteq \Gamma(X,U)$ on affine opens). Assume `I.IsInvertible`: for every point $x$ of $X$ there are an affine open $U$ and $f \in \Gamma(X,U)$ with $x \in X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\,f$ such that, on the affine open $X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\,f$, the ideal of $I$ is generated by a single non-zero-divisor $g$ of $\Gamma(X, X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\,f)$. Write $I.\mathrm{module}$ for the sheaf of modules attached to $I$ (the kernel of the map from the unit to the pushforward of the unit along the closed immersion $I.\mathrm{subscheme}\iota$), $I.\mathrm{invModule}$ for its dual, and $I.\mathrm{invModuleSection}$ for the section $\mathbf 1_X \to I.\mathrm{invModule}$ obtained by currying the inclusion $I.\mathrm{module}\iota$ of $I.\mathrm{module}$ into the unit. The theorem asserts that the zero-scheme ideal of this section equals $I$, where `zeroSchemeIdeal s` is by definition the infimum of all ideal sheaf data $J$ with $\mathrm{coeffIdeal}\,s\,U \le J.\mathrm{ideal}\,U$ for every affine open $U$, and $\mathrm{coeffIdeal}\,s\,U$ is the ideal of $\Gamma(X,U)$ generated by all values $\mathrm{coeff}\,s\,U\,\varphi$ of $s$ against $\mathcal O_U$-linear functionals $\varphi$ on the restriction of the target to $U$.
--
--   This is the statement that for an effective Cartier divisor $D$ with ideal sheaf $\mathcal O_X(-D) = \mathcal I$ the canonical section $\mathbf 1_D \colon \mathcal O_X \to \mathcal O_X(D)$, transpose of the inclusion $\mathcal I \hookrightarrow \mathcal O_X$, has zero scheme exactly $D$. It is used in the treatment of relative effective Cartier divisors and the relative Picard functor, for instance when comparing a divisor with line bundles and sections constructed from it.
